munch munch

> From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)
>    Most people who are heavily into computers, didn't get their first
> exposure to them in school. Schools rarely even present computers in
> an interesting and fun way.

Let me describe my introduction to computers, because it was in
school and it was interesting.  In fourth grade, my elementary
school had a grand total of about three Apple II+ computers that
you hooked up to the A/V department's televisions.  Not much of
an investment in hardware.

But they were able to use it effectively.  Every so often a given
class would "use the computers": all three of them would be rolled
into the room, the class would split up into three groups, and each
would have a project: to learn a LOGO construct, or to write a
program to do a particular thing.  LOGO, by the way, is one of the
better teaching languages I've seen.  You could also sign up to take
a computer home for a night or weekend to play with.

The computer shortage was alleviated when the school acquired enough
IIe's for half of a class to use at a time; programming classes then
became a regular part of the curriculum.  Except that they weren't
programming classes in any formal sense -- instead, a person or
group would make up a project, or ask for a suggestion from a
teacher, and then implement it: a drawer of Sierpinki triangles, an
arcade game, a math quiz, a pig-Latin program.  To tie into another
thread: girls did participate, and quite well.  It was long enough
ago that I don't remember any gender-related generalizations that
might be made.

This worked for me.  Why did it work?  First, we wrote programs.
Maybe it's just me, but programming is easily the most interesting
thing you can do with a computer.  Educational software is often
repetitive, condescending, slapdash in its craftsmanship, and only
marginally educational.  It often seems designed as a "teacher in a
box": a computer's cheaper than a teacher, and this way you can
afford to quiz every single student on their state capitals.
Interactively, too.  Well, <rant off>.

Second, the language used, LOGO, was a good choice.  At this level,
programming classes are mostly lessons in problem solving.  Teaching
Pascal or BASIC or something of that nature requires the student to
not only figure out how to solve the problem, but also to express it
in a language that is relatively cryptic, fussy, and clumsy with
non-atomic data.  (Mudd's intro CS course is presently taught in C++
-- and the CS department wonders why people are turned off by CS
5.)  LOGO is interpreted, allowing experimental step-by-step solution
of a problem; it also is sufficiently powerful that you won't run
into a wall later.

Third, the pedagogical technique worked.  Trying to turn a fifth
grader into a language lawyer is a bad idea.  Instead, students
learned what they needed, in order to do what they wanted to do.
This assures that they'll actually *use* what they learn, and will
likely remember it.  I never did learn how to use LOGO's
exception-handling primitives, because I never needed them.

>    IMHO, the majority of kids exposed to computers nowadays get that
> exposure and interest from _video games_.  There are quite a few people
> who buy PCs/Amigas to simply play games.

Unfortunately, these days they more likely buy a Nintendo/whatever
is hot these days.  Game machines are cheaper than PCs, and
typically have more and flashier games.  Plus, "all my friends have
a [whatever]".  Unfortunately, non-game apps for game machines are
few and far between, and third-party development systems are AFAIK
non-existent.  To apply your example of the net as a major time
sink, game machines are like net accounts that can do nothing but
plya DikuMUD.

> -- Ray Cromwell

