Date: Thu, 9 Dec 93 20:29:34 PST
From: Eli Brandt <ebrandt@jarthur.Claremont.EDU>
Subject: Women in some technical professions:  Observations

An observation on women in science: at Harvey Mudd, the gender ratio
is pretty bad (from my perspective); about 20% of students are
female.  However, perhaps 40% of the chemistry majors are women, as
are 3 of the 9 chemistry professors.  This compares to perhaps 5%
and 0 of 15 for mathematics (but no, that's not why I take chemistry
classes).

I can't explain this, but it looks like more than a statistical
fluke.  It suggests to me that the much-discussed "deficit" of women
in technical professions may be due to environmental factors which
are absent in our chemistry department, or over-present in our other
departments.  Perhaps an all-male faculty really is intimidating, or
maybe it's something else.

   Eli   ebrandt@jarthur.claremont.edu

