I was using it to refer simply to standard NMR techniques with high
spatial resolution.  "Some guys gave a talk" in which they showed
some images made with ~10um resolution, and talked about getting it
down to 1um.  This is pretty small.  You wouldn't need that unless
you train yourself to control a single neuron, I don't think.
Medical MRI technology runs about millimeter rez, I think, and "Fast
NMR" techniques based on this are pretty hot in brain research these
days.

There was recently a post on sci.nanotech about "NMR microscopy"
involving a scanning-prope microscope hacked to do NMR as well.  So
maybe the term is already taken.  They were talking about possibly
resolving individual atoms.  This would inevitably be an invasive
technique, though...  (I wasn't clear on how they planned to do NMR
on, say, 14C atoms.  Anybody get this?)

