First off, I don't think much about this so-called "Singularity."
Maybe something like it will happen in 50 or 100 years, maybe not. In
any case, my time horizon is much shorter and inolves decidecly
non-Singularity things.

I of course expect the elite, the educated, those who strive, to
greatly "pull away" from the pack behind. The uneducated and uncaring
louts--we may as call them "proles"--will have a rising standard of
living, too, but will be far, far behind the elite. And the proles
will use democracy to try to steal the assets of the elite.

Hence, Extropians reject democracy out of hand.

> Is this widening gap the *primary* reason that you're an
> Extropian?  Is what you like the idea that you're getting
> farther and farther from the masses, or is it the possibilities
> intrinsic to the heights you hope to reach?
> 
> Suppose that intelligence increase technology gets to the point
> that there are no masses any more--just Jupiter Brains doing
> cool Jupiter Brain stuff. Would this be a disappointment to you?

I don't of course buy the Jupiter-sized brain theory. (It started out,
as someone just noted, as a tag line for an interesting speculation
but is in danger now of becoming one of the core Extropian beliefs,
which I think would be unfortunate.)

My reason for wanting to see my capabilities and wealth increase is
mostly selfish: more degrees of freedom, more interesting things to
do. As long as enough peoole join me, I'll be happy.

What most of the planet's human biomass, all 300E9 kilos of it, does
is not my chief concern. I can't get worried in the abstract about the
lives or deaths of people who most certainly don't care about me.

