Nancy Lebovitz asks:

>Cryptoanarchy would probably make it easier to get false exit
>permits and such, but it doesn't seem like it's enough to
>offer much protection against indiscrimminate attacks--does
>anyone have ideas (whether computer-based or not) for what
>might work to avoid or stop a waco government?
>

and at the risk of stating the obvious I offer:

   Whatever steps are taken to stop, avoid or change waco governments or
waco government actions, will probably have been discussed privately among
concerned citizens. Among more and better-informed concerned citizens if
the citizens have digital privacy.

   The privacy question pretty much has to go one of two ways; either we
have privacy or not. There's not much middle ground, less if you take into
account the long term effects of dropping computing costs and human
ingenuity. 

   Either as a society we treat each other as responsible adults, who's
privacy is respected or those in power treat the rest of the citizens as
untrustworthy potential criminals.I think whichever way the public policy
goes is likely to be a self fulfilling prophecy. 


