>
>Whether it's kiosks or spoken words is beside the point--the point that
>the "literate" open their minds up to a world of possibilities that
>the illiterat, innumerate, and uncaring cannot have access to.

OK--that's where the disagreement is--you've got illiterate, innumerate,
and uncaring packaged as one concept, and I don't. I don't know about
the typical illiterate person, but I've heard of an illiterate man
who has his own company--he found that he couldn't get any farther
than draughtsman as an employee, and he invented a device for
measuring the depth of grooves optically and much more accurately
than any other machine on the market. Why couldn't he read? I don't
know if he'd run afoul of incredibly bad teaching or had a neurological
problem, but he was obviously neither innumerate nor uncaring.

I've met a man who simply never read after he got out of school because
reading gave him horrible headaches--it doesn't seem to me that he
was some sort of intrinsically inferior person.

>The ignorant cannot hope to have enough "mechanical aids" to explain
>to them what they have no conception of. They literally won't even
>know the knowledge exists, and hence will not ask for it. Nor will
>they care that they are ignorant.

I work at home and listen to NPR (National Public Radio) a lot. I'd
prefer something a tidge more libertarian, but even so, NPR is
the best station I know of for an information junky--and it--or
something like it, would supply a starting point for someone 
illiterate to find out what might be interesting or useful. 

I'm certainly not arguing that most illiterate people are thirsting
for knowledge, but then, most literate people don't seem to be,
either, and I'd rather see such smart illiterates as exist have
access to knowledge.

In other words, I'm speaking for potential members of the elite.

I consider it entirely possible that illiterate people can do a
great deal more than they seem able to, simply because illiteracy
is so despised. This means that it's hard for them to learn, hard
for them to get jobs that they're qualified for, and if they
do rise in the world (like the company president that I mentioned),
they do it by concealling their illiteracy and thus aren't counted
as illiterates. 

>The bums and winos who inhabit the local libraries during the day,
>scaring many parents into keeping their own children away, are not
>using the "knowledge" contained therein to pull themselves out of the
>gutter. They _like_ living the way they do.

I actually don't know how many people remain homeless permanently--
does anyone have any information or numbers?

In any case, at that point, we're not just talking about people who
can't read--those bums may be able to read, anyway. We're talking
about people who are probably alcoholics or crazy, which is a 
whole additional difficulty on top of lack of literacy.

Furthermore, now you've gone from considering mere illiteracy to
problems with lack of initiative and inability to connect information
to one's goals.

